WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:127826

1
00:00:02.135 --> 00:00:04.204
You can skip this lesson if you don't care

2
00:00:04.204 --> 00:00:07.207
about the quality of the retrieval
in your RAG application.

3
00:00:07.807 --> 00:00:11.044
If you stay, you will learn the tools
and the most commonly used

4
00:00:11.044 --> 00:00:14.047
metrics of the information retrieval.

5
00:00:14.047 --> 00:00:16.583
You can't improve what you don't measure.

6
00:00:16.583 --> 00:00:19.219
So we need to learn how to measure it.

7
00:00:19.219 --> 00:00:22.288
Before we discuss optimizations.
Let's get to it.

8
00:00:23.523 --> 00:00:27.560
Measuring the quality of the system
outputs is the first thing to do

9
00:00:27.594 --> 00:00:29.029
if you want to optimize it.

10
00:00:29.029 --> 00:00:31.831
How else could you know
that your application works better

11
00:00:31.831 --> 00:00:33.933
after doing some changes?

12
00:00:33.933 --> 00:00:37.303
Such relevance is not a new area,
as we've been measuring

13
00:00:37.303 --> 00:00:40.306
how well the search engines
behave for decades.

14
00:00:40.874 --> 00:00:43.877
The same means might be applied
to semantic search

15
00:00:44.010 --> 00:00:46.980
as it's just a new approach
to information retrieval.

16
00:00:47.180 --> 00:00:50.817
Let's review
the most common metrics used in that space

17
00:00:50.817 --> 00:00:52.452
and see how we can apply them.

18
00:00:52.452 --> 00:00:55.055
For example,
in retrieval augmented generation.

19
00:00:55.055 --> 00:00:58.458
Everything starts with building
a reference ground truth datasets.

20
00:00:58.892 --> 00:01:03.163
It contains a set of queries and the best
matching documents for each of them.

21
00:01:03.530 --> 00:01:06.533
Building such a dataset
is a real challenge as it requires

22
00:01:06.533 --> 00:01:07.967
lots of human labor.

23
00:01:07.967 --> 00:01:11.404
Moreover,
the real intents of a particular person

24
00:01:11.404 --> 00:01:15.842
are rarely perfectly reflected
in the queries they write, and different

25
00:01:15.842 --> 00:01:19.379
items may be more relevant
for different groups of people

26
00:01:19.712 --> 00:01:23.983
even if the query is the same.
Even though a smaller yet

27
00:01:23.983 --> 00:01:28.788
well-curated ground truth dataset
is a sign of maturity of any project,

28
00:01:28.788 --> 00:01:33.093
it helps to track how your changes
improve the quality of the results.

29
00:01:33.293 --> 00:01:37.897
There are two typical ways of annotating
the relevance of a document given query.

30
00:01:38.331 --> 00:01:43.331
Binary item is either
relevant or not, or numerical.

31
00:01:43.770 --> 00:01:47.107
We express the relevance of an item
as a number.

32
00:01:47.240 --> 00:01:51.277
The higher it is, the more relevant
a particular document is.

33
00:01:51.644 --> 00:01:56.182
Obviously, for each query,
the set of relevant results differs.

34
00:01:56.516 --> 00:02:00.420
Since the process of creating a dataset
requires lots of efforts,

35
00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:04.924
we will just use one of the existing
datasets which already has the ground

36
00:02:04.924 --> 00:02:06.092
truth defined.

37
00:02:06.092 --> 00:02:10.130
Wafair Annotation Dataset is an example
you are going to use.

38
00:02:10.663 --> 00:02:12.932
Let's check how it looks like.

39
00:02:12.932 --> 00:02:15.935
WANDS stands for Wafair Annotation Data Set.

40
00:02:16.102 --> 00:02:21.007
It is a benchmark to determine the
effectiveness of different search methods.

41
00:02:21.407 --> 00:02:24.644
It provides multiple CSV files,
including the products.

42
00:02:25.145 --> 00:02:27.280
Each product has multiple parameters,

43
00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:30.783
but for semantic search, product
name and description

44
00:02:31.084 --> 00:02:35.588
seem both to be natural candidates
for being encoded by the embedding model.

45
00:02:36.523 --> 00:02:40.360
In this lesson, we are going to build
two search processes.

46
00:02:41.027 --> 00:02:44.664
One of them will be searching
over product names and the other one

47
00:02:44.664 --> 00:02:45.965
on the descriptions.

48
00:02:45.965 --> 00:02:49.402
Eventually, you will measure
which one works better.

49
00:02:49.802 --> 00:02:53.072
First of all, let's convert
the product names into vectors.

50
00:02:53.072 --> 00:02:58.072
We are not going to convert all of them,
but just a subset of the data,

51
00:02:59.412 --> 00:03:03.149
and num products will define
how many you want to select.

52
00:03:04.083 --> 00:03:05.818
You will use the same

53
00:03:05.818 --> 00:03:09.189
sentence transformer
to calculate the embeddings

54
00:03:09.656 --> 00:03:13.293
of the product names
for the selected number of them.

55
00:03:13.626 --> 00:03:17.730
This process took some time,
and if you would like to do it

56
00:03:17.730 --> 00:03:20.466
for the whole dataset,
it will be even longer.

57
00:03:20.466 --> 00:03:24.370
But each product name is now vectorized.
As a next step,

58
00:03:24.370 --> 00:03:27.373
let's do the same for the descriptions.

59
00:03:29.509 --> 00:03:31.177
The process takes even longer

60
00:03:31.177 --> 00:03:35.215
for the descriptions because those texts
are supposed to be longer.

61
00:03:35.381 --> 00:03:38.384
And if you remember
how the embedding models work,

62
00:03:38.851 --> 00:03:41.221
they take a sequence of tokens,

63
00:03:41.221 --> 00:03:45.491
and the more tokens we have, the longer
the processing will be.

64
00:03:46.025 --> 00:03:50.463
You could also experiment and combine
the product name with its description,

65
00:03:50.797 --> 00:03:54.500
but here, you will just use
and test these two ways.

66
00:03:54.934 --> 00:03:58.304
Searching over our dataset requires
building a collection

67
00:03:58.304 --> 00:03:59.906
in the vector database.

68
00:03:59.906 --> 00:04:04.844
You will name it WANDS-products,
and it will have two vectors

69
00:04:04.844 --> 00:04:09.148
for each point, one for the name
and the other one for the description.

70
00:04:15.021 --> 00:04:18.324
The collection you created is still empty,
but you will fill it

71
00:04:18.491 --> 00:04:19.659
with data right away.

72
00:04:20.793 --> 00:04:22.395
Our dataframe might be

73
00:04:22.395 --> 00:04:25.398
converted into dictionaries,

74
00:04:25.531 --> 00:04:30.531
and its index will be used as identifiers
of points, as the identifiers

75
00:04:31.371 --> 00:04:34.807
might be either integers
or UUID-like strings.

76
00:04:35.642 --> 00:04:37.844
The upload collection method will iterate

77
00:04:37.844 --> 00:04:40.847
in batches of 64 elements at a time.

78
00:04:40.913 --> 00:04:42.448
Once the upload is finished,

79
00:04:42.448 --> 00:04:46.252
our collection already has all
the products ready to be searched over,

80
00:04:46.586 --> 00:04:49.589
and we can check
the number of points in it.

81
00:04:51.424 --> 00:04:52.625
That doesn't mean

82
00:04:52.625 --> 00:04:55.862
all the internal processes
have finished already.

83
00:04:56.362 --> 00:04:59.832
Vector databases implement
an approximation of nearest neighbor

84
00:04:59.832 --> 00:05:03.903
search, and the optimizer has to build
some helper data structures

85
00:05:03.903 --> 00:05:05.838
to make search efficient.

86
00:05:05.838 --> 00:05:07.840
Let's wait

87
00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:11.244
until the collection status becomes
green as it means

88
00:05:11.377 --> 00:05:14.380
the data is indexed already.

89
00:05:17.650 --> 00:05:22.121
Another part of the WAND's
dataset is a set of test queries.

90
00:05:23.389 --> 00:05:24.090
This query

91
00:05:24.090 --> 00:05:27.093
still do not define the ideal matches.

92
00:05:27.260 --> 00:05:30.263
That's what is put in the third file.

93
00:05:32.298 --> 00:05:34.000
The ground truth dataset

94
00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:36.969
maps queries to the relevant documents.

95
00:05:38.104 --> 00:05:41.407
Each entry has a label
which is their exact,

96
00:05:42.108 --> 00:05:44.544
partial, or irrelevant.

97
00:05:44.544 --> 00:05:48.214
It's always easier to define the relevancy
as a number,

98
00:05:48.247 --> 00:05:51.250
so let's convert texts into scores

99
00:05:51.551 --> 00:05:53.886
by a simple mapping.

100
00:05:53.886 --> 00:05:56.522
In addition to that, query and document id's

101
00:05:56.522 --> 00:05:59.992
will get a prefix
to distinguish them from each other.

102
00:06:04.297 --> 00:06:07.300
We didn't mention how to measure
quality yet.

103
00:06:07.333 --> 00:06:11.137
Information retrieval uses different types
of metrics and we will review them now.

104
00:06:11.504 --> 00:06:16.504
Each query should result in returning
a set of responses sorted by relevance.

105
00:06:16.776 --> 00:06:20.680
When it comes to vector search,
measuring quality comes quite naturally.

106
00:06:20.880 --> 00:06:23.816
Our distance function, such as cosine
distance,

107
00:06:23.816 --> 00:06:27.954
is used to sort the documents
our system finds to be the most relevant.

108
00:06:28.221 --> 00:06:31.991
In the words of information retrieval,
we would say they are ranked-based

109
00:06:32.125 --> 00:06:33.760
on the similarity.

110
00:06:33.760 --> 00:06:35.962
There are mainly three kinds of metrics

111
00:06:35.962 --> 00:06:38.898
you can calculate. Relevancy based,

112
00:06:38.898 --> 00:06:42.068
which only care
if a certain document is relevant or not.

113
00:06:42.235 --> 00:06:45.538
Those metrics are useful
if our ground truth dataset is binary.

114
00:06:45.538 --> 00:06:49.108
So we don't know how relevant
each document is.

115
00:06:49.409 --> 00:06:53.346
There are also ranking-related metrics
which take care of the position

116
00:06:53.346 --> 00:06:56.349
of the relevant items in the results.

117
00:06:56.649 --> 00:06:59.919
Intuitively, we want to put the best items

118
00:06:59.919 --> 00:07:03.256
to the top of the list
and not present them on the third page.

119
00:07:03.756 --> 00:07:08.428
The last group focuses not only on
the fact of being relevant, but also takes

120
00:07:08.428 --> 00:07:12.498
the relevancy score from the ground
truth dataset into consideration.

121
00:07:12.899 --> 00:07:15.802
Let's discuss the first group of metrics.

122
00:07:15.802 --> 00:07:20.173
A common way to calculate the quality
of the search system is precision@K.

123
00:07:20.873 --> 00:07:23.810
This metric measures
the fraction of relevant items

124
00:07:23.810 --> 00:07:26.813
in the top
K results returned by our system.

125
00:07:27.146 --> 00:07:30.149
If we return fewer than K relevant
documents,

126
00:07:30.383 --> 00:07:33.386
achieving
a 100% score might be impossible,

127
00:07:33.519 --> 00:07:36.956
but still, that's a good way to compare
different search pipelines.

128
00:07:36.989 --> 00:07:41.989
Precision at K is calculated per query,
and we usually report an average precision

129
00:07:42.462 --> 00:07:46.466
at k for all the queries from the ground
truth dataset.

130
00:07:46.666 --> 00:07:51.270
Similarly, recall that k measures
how much of the relevant documents

131
00:07:51.437 --> 00:07:54.140
we can return in the top k results.

132
00:07:54.140 --> 00:07:54.807
Perfectly,

133
00:07:54.807 --> 00:07:59.078
we would be returning 100% of them,
but that might be hard

134
00:07:59.078 --> 00:08:02.181
if there are more than k
relevant documents per query.

135
00:08:02.782 --> 00:08:07.782
Again, we usually report the average
we call at k for all the queries.

136
00:08:08.387 --> 00:08:12.191
It's rather common to present
both precision and recall together.

137
00:08:12.758 --> 00:08:15.995
Another group of metric incorporates
the information

138
00:08:15.995 --> 00:08:18.030
about the ranking of the document.

139
00:08:18.030 --> 00:08:22.068
In other words,
the order of the documents matters.

140
00:08:22.068 --> 00:08:26.739
Mean Reciprocal Rank is a commonly used metric
that belongs to this group.

141
00:08:27.340 --> 00:08:31.210
It does not care
about the number of relevant items returned,

142
00:08:31.477 --> 00:08:34.914
but only considers the position
of the first relevant one.

143
00:08:35.214 --> 00:08:37.083
You will probably choose to optimize

144
00:08:37.083 --> 00:08:40.586
for that metric
if you want to present something relevant

145
00:08:41.020 --> 00:08:43.122
in the very top of the results.

146
00:08:43.122 --> 00:08:46.359
It is a well-known fact
that the first position of the Google

147
00:08:46.359 --> 00:08:49.729
search
results are also the most clicked ones.

148
00:08:50.296 --> 00:08:54.834
Last but not least, Score related
metrics are also commonly used.

149
00:08:55.201 --> 00:08:58.204
Discounted
Cumulative Gain is one of the examples.

150
00:08:58.404 --> 00:09:02.208
It measures the total relevance
of the return documents,

151
00:09:02.441 --> 00:09:06.812
but also addresses the problem
of diminishing relevance of items

152
00:09:06.812 --> 00:09:08.414
down the results list.

153
00:09:08.414 --> 00:09:11.250
There are different alternative
formulations of how

154
00:09:11.250 --> 00:09:13.119
to calculate DCG,

155
00:09:13.119 --> 00:09:15.588
but the main idea stays the same.

156
00:09:15.588 --> 00:09:19.025
We want to promote the fact
of putting relevant

157
00:09:19.025 --> 00:09:23.462
items in the top of the results.
The value of DCG at k

158
00:09:23.462 --> 00:09:27.800
is not normalized,
so we quite often divided by IDCG

159
00:09:28.000 --> 00:09:31.404
which stands for Ideal
Discounted Cumulative Gain.

160
00:09:32.238 --> 00:09:35.741
This is a DCG
that the perfect ranking would get.

161
00:09:37.009 --> 00:09:37.843
That gives us a

162
00:09:37.843 --> 00:09:41.147
value which
is normalized to the range of 0 to 1.

163
00:09:41.614 --> 00:09:44.250
We could possibly calculate
the metrics on our own,

164
00:09:44.250 --> 00:09:48.154
but it doesn't make much sense as
there are already some existing libraries.

165
00:09:48.454 --> 00:09:52.091
Ranx is one of them,
and it's fairly easy to use.

166
00:09:52.792 --> 00:09:56.162
There are two main concepts: Qrels

167
00:09:56.295 --> 00:10:00.733
query relevance judgments a.k.a our ground
truth. And Run,

168
00:10:01.000 --> 00:10:04.437
which are the outputs
as returned by our search systems.

169
00:10:04.570 --> 00:10:09.570
Generally, for each query we defined
a set of documents and their importance.

170
00:10:10.643 --> 00:10:14.347
In the case of Qrels
the importance is an integer.

171
00:10:14.447 --> 00:10:17.083
The higher the better to match.

172
00:10:17.083 --> 00:10:21.654
For runs, we just use the similarity
scores as returned by the vector db.

173
00:10:21.787 --> 00:10:24.724
Let's come back to the words dataset
and see how

174
00:10:24.724 --> 00:10:28.294
well different attempts
solve the problem of search.

175
00:10:28.861 --> 00:10:31.530
You should rather avoid implementing
the metrics on your own.

176
00:10:32.565 --> 00:10:32.898
Let's

177
00:10:32.898 --> 00:10:36.702
use ranx and define the Qrels object
from the ground

178
00:10:36.702 --> 00:10:39.705
truth dataset.

179
00:10:41.974 --> 00:10:44.710
You will now convert
all the queries into embeddings

180
00:10:44.710 --> 00:10:47.713
so you can use them in their evaluations.

181
00:10:54.887 --> 00:10:58.157
Ranx requires a run object that describes

182
00:10:58.157 --> 00:11:01.627
the set of results returned
for a particular query.

183
00:11:02.495 --> 00:11:07.299
Each returned document should get the
score assigned by the search mechanism.

184
00:11:07.900 --> 00:11:09.068
You will do it twice.

185
00:11:09.068 --> 00:11:12.104
First of all, searching over the product

186
00:11:12.104 --> 00:11:15.107
name. We'll define a dictionary

187
00:11:15.341 --> 00:11:18.944
that will keep the run object data.

188
00:11:20.413 --> 00:11:22.415
For that, we need to iterate over

189
00:11:22.415 --> 00:11:25.418
all the queries from the dataset

190
00:11:25.451 --> 00:11:30.322
and then run a search operation
on the collection we created before.

191
00:11:31.057 --> 00:11:33.759
We are going to use the product name

192
00:11:33.759 --> 00:11:37.029
and use the query
embedding created in the previous cell.

193
00:11:37.563 --> 00:11:42.068
Once we have the set of results
as returned by the vector database,

194
00:11:42.401 --> 00:11:46.072
we have to iterate over
the points returned

195
00:11:46.839 --> 00:11:51.644
and build the dictionary structure
as requested by ranx.

196
00:11:52.144 --> 00:11:55.481
Let's also display it
so we know how it looks like.

197
00:12:05.124 --> 00:12:05.991
Now, this

198
00:12:05.991 --> 00:12:09.128
dictionary has to be converted
into run object.

199
00:12:10.129 --> 00:12:11.263
You can do the same,

200
00:12:11.263 --> 00:12:14.266
but for the product descriptions.

201
00:12:15.134 --> 00:12:19.271
Another run object will encode the results
for this attempt.

202
00:12:20.740 --> 00:12:24.477
In the final step,
you will compare both search pipelines.

203
00:12:25.311 --> 00:12:28.581
Ranx allows to pass a list of metrics

204
00:12:28.981 --> 00:12:31.784
you want to measure as a list of strings.

205
00:12:31.784 --> 00:12:34.120
In our case, we want the precision,

206
00:12:34.120 --> 00:12:37.123
recall, mrr, DCG, ndcg

207
00:12:37.123 --> 00:12:41.160
and this all of them at k equal ten.

208
00:12:47.833 --> 00:12:50.169
All the metrics suggest

209
00:12:50.169 --> 00:12:53.405
the product name is a better candidate
for the semantic search.

210
00:12:54.173 --> 00:12:58.644
Don't be surprised if your test returns
slightly different values of this metrics,

211
00:12:58.878 --> 00:12:59.979
as vector databases

212
00:12:59.979 --> 00:13:03.382
may build their internal data structures
differently on each of them.

213
00:13:03.849 --> 00:13:05.217
You might be still wondering

214
00:13:05.217 --> 00:13:08.888
how measuring the quality might improve
your retrieval

215
00:13:08.921 --> 00:13:10.823
augmented generation apps.

216
00:13:10.823 --> 00:13:13.793
Measuring the quality of retrieval
is an important thing

217
00:13:13.793 --> 00:13:15.628
in the retrieval argument generation.

218
00:13:15.628 --> 00:13:19.865
The idea of putting relevant information
into the prompt improves

219
00:13:19.865 --> 00:13:24.403
the ability of the large language models
to work with our private data.

220
00:13:24.804 --> 00:13:29.375
Our prompts won't help if we fill them
with non-essential information.

221
00:13:29.842 --> 00:13:33.412
However, if our search mechanism is unable
to provide

222
00:13:33.412 --> 00:13:37.483
the LLM with anything
meaningful, the whole process will fail.

223
00:13:37.550 --> 00:13:41.020
That's why it's so important to test it
carefully.

224
00:13:41.620 --> 00:13:44.290
Moreover, testing the retrieval is way

225
00:13:44.290 --> 00:13:47.860
more simpler than testing the whole RAG
pipeline end to end.

226
00:13:48.360 --> 00:13:52.231
As the latter usually involves
another LLM as a judge.

227
00:13:52.698 --> 00:13:56.435
Information retrieval is nothing new,
and there are ways of making

228
00:13:56.435 --> 00:13:59.438
sure it returns what it's expected to do.

229
00:13:59.471 --> 00:14:02.074
If you take your project seriously,
you should

230
00:14:02.074 --> 00:14:05.411
consider building a reference dataset
from the very beginning.

231
00:14:05.578 --> 00:14:09.882
It helps to avoid some common pitfalls
of building AI applications

232
00:14:09.882 --> 00:14:11.283
with semantic search.

233
00:14:11.283 --> 00:14:16.155
Think of it as if it was just another
set of test cases you run in the CI

234
00:14:16.155 --> 00:14:20.893
pipeline to make sure your search results
do not get worse over time.

235
00:14:21.493 --> 00:14:25.831
In this lesson, you learned how to measure
the quality of the retrieval systems.

236
00:14:26.298 --> 00:14:29.535
If you want to serve
a different number of results, check out

237
00:14:29.635 --> 00:14:33.539
how changing the k parameter will change
the values of different metrics.

238
00:14:33.806 --> 00:14:36.609
In the next lesson, you will learn

239
00:14:36.609 --> 00:14:39.745
how to use these quality measures
to optimize

240
00:14:39.745 --> 00:14:43.916
the approximate nearest neighbor
search in your vector database.

241
00:14:44.550 --> 00:14:45.217
See you there!

